many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism what is the  most important idea in stoicism this is  a perfectly natural question but it  doesn't have a good answer in part  because somebody's asking for what can't  really be provided now of course anybody  can pull an idea out of the air and say  this is the most important one but if  you read your stoic texts and you think  through how Stoics actually live you can  see that there isn't one single most  important idea  there's instead an entire framework of  ideas and if you try to oversimplify you  actually start to not only lose the  efficacy of the stoic approach but you  start to misunderstand it and get into a  number of quandary  and paradoxes before I introduce you to  a few of the candidates for the most  important idea and stoicism the things  that people often put forward as the one  central fundamental idea I want to talk  about this this notion that there should  in fact be one key insight one key  practice one kernel that everything else  would somehow grow out of and I used a  metaphor there of growth you know like a  seed stoicism doesn't work that way  philosophies in general don't work that  way and that's part of what's good about  them it's not just about having one idea  and then driving that thing into the  ground it's about putting multiple ideas  together which is a much more powerful  approach in part because reality out  there is a hell of a lot more  complicated than one idea can do justice  to especially when we're taking account  of the reality in here  of the human being and human nature some  of the big ideas that often get proposed  as the most important or the most  significant idea concept practice  what-have-you in stoicism are the  following one is what's called the  dichotomy of control some things are in  our control other things are not in our  control and we want to get that idea  straight that distinction so we can  apply it properly another is that virtue  is the only true good and correlative  lis vise is the only true evil another  idea is that we human beings are not  simply stuck with or the prey of our  thoughts desires emotions we can have  some measure of choice and control when  it comes to those a fourth key idea is  that the human being is fundamentally a  rational animal and that this involves  us within an entire social matrix of  other people with whom we have  relationships and roles and those  influence how our life goes another key  idea is that it's not events or persons  or whatever else is coming at us from  the outside that makes us suffer but  rather our own judgments and attitudes  about those events and persons yet  another idea that that is really at the  core stoicism is that the entire  universe including our you know  microcosm within is fundamentally  rational and that much of the problems  that we we encounter and suffer from  have to do from a bad fit between  ourselves and the rationality of the  whole and then a final idea that I think  often gets brought up very calm  Lexx idea by itself is that we ought to  be living in accordance with nature now  each of these gets put forth by some  people as being the one most important  idea of Stoicism each of these ideas  that I've introduced you to is a main  idea of stoic philosophy some of them  actually were not articulated as such  until fairly late like the dichotomy of  control  but that's a very important one and  instead of trying to say which of these  is the one that I have to concentrate on  you should think of it as a set of ideas  that connect up with each other and help  to inform each other sort of like a net  or a network if you like composed of a  bunch of knots or nodes in which that  composes the fabric of the whole and  that's what gives it its strength you  don't want to be the sort of person who  has one idea and that ignores all of the  rest because if you do that you're not  really getting that much even out of  that very idea and you won't be  connecting it with the other key ideas  of stoic philosophy so it's great if  you're asking this question but you  really want to reformulate it into what  are the most important ideas about  stoicism and then you want to follow it  up with where do I go to actually learn  about these if you found this answer  helpful for you then please share it  with others who might benefit from it  this work is entirely supported by  crowdfunding on my patreon site so if  you find it valuable consider becoming a  supporter at patreon.com slash Saddler
 

